/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  outerDict -- WHERE THE THREE CATALYST VOLUMES AND THE CONVERTER'S ONE
  DIAMETER ARE COMPUTED (DEV.md 4c C66, C69).

  The `pfr` is a RATING model: it integrates the volume it is given.  A basic
  design SIZES the bed instead -- it fixes a criterion and finds the volume
  that meets it.  This DesignSpec does that, for all three beds AT ONCE:

      $V_Bed1  ->  CONVERTER.Bed1.T_out      = 788.15 K  (515 C: 5 K under the 520 C
                                                  the base data give as the
                                                  catalyst's limit)
      $V_Bed2  ->  CONVERTER.Bed2.approach_K = 5 K       (US 5,352,428, Bhakta &
      $V_Bed3  ->  CONVERTER.Bed3.approach_K = 5 K        Grotz 1994: "the effluent
                                                  from each bed has an
                                                  approach to equilibrium of
                                                  5 C")
      $D_converter -> the LARGEST of the three beds' dP_over_allowable = 1
                                                  (C69)

  THE FOURTH UNKNOWN IS THE CONVERTER'S DIAMETER.  The three beds sit in ONE
  shell and so share ONE diameter (each bed reads `diameter $D_converter;`),
  and the diameter is the SMALLEST at which every bed's Ergun drop is within
  its declared allowable (1 bar, CONVERTER/Bed1's header).  Each drop falls
  monotonically as the converter widens (at a fixed volume u and L both go
  as 1/D^2), so the LARGEST of the three drops over allowable falls
  monotonically too, and the smallest admissible diameter is the one at
  which that largest sits exactly at 1.  The target says exactly that
  (`largestOf`), so no bed is named in advance as the binding one: the run
  names the one that binds at the design point (bed 3 here -- at one
  diameter the longest bed is the largest volume).  The converterShell sizer
  then brackets the same smallest diameter on the solved beds and says
  whether the converter's sits at it.  Solved inside this Newton rather than
  bracketed around it: a 1-D bracket would re-run the whole 3 x 3 DesignSpec
  at every trial diameter, and the drop is smooth and monotone in D.  The
  one non-smoothness is max() itself, where the largest changes hands; it
  is not at this answer (the run says so if it ever is).

  SIMULTANEOUS, NOT SEQUENTIAL, and why that is the honest reading.  The
  DesignSpec is ONE Newton on n case variables against n targets, its
  Jacobian by finite differences (src/outerDriver/DesignSpec.cpp) -- there is
  no "one spec per variable" mode.  Here the coupling is real: bed 1's volume
  moves its outlet, the interbed cooler lands bed 2's inlet at a FIXED 420 C
  whatever comes in, but bed 1's conversion still moves bed 2's composition
  and so its approach; and bed 3's effluent closes the FEHE energy recycle
  that sets bed 1's inlet.  So the 3 x 3 Jacobian is lower-triangular only
  approximately, and solving the three together is what the loop requires.
  Each residual is scaled by its own `tol`, so `tolF 1` is "every target
  within its tolerance".

  ONE starting point, not two: each `initial` below equals the value in
  system/flowsheetDict's `variables {}`, and both are the design point this
  Newton last converged to (with the Ergun pressure drop on, from the
  constant-pressure design point 8.7238 / 18.2281 / 31.4450 m3, in two
  Newton steps and fifteen evaluations; the hand-tuned 8.72 / 18.23 /
  31.44 m3 the case carried before C66 had reached that one in one step; with
  the fourth unknown, C69, from 8.8861 / 18.4225 / 31.7447 m3 and 2.26 m, in
  two Newton steps and nineteen evaluations).  So a
  run without this file RATES the designed beds, and a group that changes
  anything upstream re-solves them by running the case.  The post-processing chain (sizing, relief valves,
  costing, economics) runs once, on the replay at the design point --
  `DesignSpec::postDictPolicy()`, said on the run header.

  WHAT IS NOT SOLVED HERE, and stays a declared decision: the two interbed
  outlet temperatures (420 and 410 C) and the recycle compressor's power.  A
  designer would optimise the interbed temperatures against catalyst volume;
  that is an optimisation, not a specification, and it is not this file.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

type designSpec;

manipulate
(
    //  Bounds are advisory clips on the Newton step, not physics: below the
    //  lower bound a bed is so short its approach is hundreds of kelvin and
    //  the residual flat.
    { variable V_Bed1;   initial  8.9510; min 2.0;   max  40.0; }   // m3 of catalyst bed
    { variable V_Bed2;   initial 18.4115; min 4.0;   max  80.0; }
    { variable V_Bed3;   initial 31.6971; min 6.0;   max 150.0; }
    { variable D_converter; initial 2.2283; min 1.5; max  4.0; }   // m, the converter's one diameter
);

targets
(
    { path CONVERTER.Bed1.T_out;        value 788.15;   tol 0.02; }   // K -- 515 C
    { path CONVERTER.Bed2.approach_K;   value 5.0;      tol 0.02; }   // K, US 5,352,428 per-bed approach
    { path CONVERTER.Bed3.approach_K;   value 5.0;      tol 0.02; }
    //  The LARGEST of the beds' drops over allowable at 1: the smallest
    //  diameter at which every bed is within its allowable (C69).
    { largestOf ( CONVERTER.Bed1.dP_over_allowable
                  CONVERTER.Bed2.dP_over_allowable
                  CONVERTER.Bed3.dP_over_allowable );
      value 1.0;   tol 0.002; }
);

options
{
    maxIter      40;
    tolF         1.0;        // |F|_2 < 1: every residual within its own tol
    fdStep       1.0e-3;
}

report
{
    file     designspec_history.csv;
}
